    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The kingdom of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, which grew from small beginnings in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">Thames</a> valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-solent">Solent</a> region.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> it alone of the English kingdoms survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> onslaught, defended by a network of fortified burhs.</li><li>Alfred’s heirs conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, and in 927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> made Wessex the core of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, first compiled in Wessex in Alfred’s reign, was still being kept up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough-abbey">Peterborough Abbey</a> in 1154.</p></aside>
    </section>

