    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cerdic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cerdic.jpg" alt="Cerdic" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cherdik_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Speed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a></em>, compiled in Wessex in the late ninth century, the kingdom was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynric">Cynric</a>, who landed on the south coast with five ships in 495 and in 519 ‘took the kingdom’ after a battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charford">Cerdicesford</a>, probably Charford on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-avon-hampshire">Avon</a>. Historians treat this account with caution: its dates appear to have been artificially arranged, Cerdic’s name is British rather than Germanic, and the story was written to give the ruling dynasty a heroic origin. Archaeology and other sources suggest that the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, known early on as the Gewisse, formed first in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">Thames</a> valley around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames">Dorchester-on-Thames</a>, where early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> cemeteries are numerous, and that groups in the Thames valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-solent">Solent</a> region came together under a single dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceawlin-of-wessex">Ceawlin</a>, who ruled in the later sixth century and was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> among the kings who held authority over the southern English, expanded westwards; the <em>Chronicle</em> records his victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dyrham">Battle of Dyrham</a> in 577, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirencester">Cirencester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> under West Saxon control. In the seventh century, under pressure from Mercia in the Thames valley, the kingdom’s centre shifted south and west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> became its principal ecclesiastical and royal centre.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winchester"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winchester.jpg" alt="Winchester" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winchester_Montage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">(CC BY-SA 2.0), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wessex was a farming kingdom of chalk downland, fertile river valleys and, in the west, the heavier lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>. Most people lived in villages and hamlets, growing wheat and barley and keeping sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and society ranged from nobles, the <em>gesiths</em> and later thegns, through free peasants, or ceorls, to slaves. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine</a>’s laws give Britons lower wergilds than Englishmen, showing that a large British population lived under West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> arrived when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birinus">Birinus</a> baptised King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynegils">Cynegils</a> in 635, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a> as godfather, and established a see at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorchester-on-thames">Dorchester-on-Thames</a>; the see later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, and a second bishopric was created at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sherborne">Sherborne</a> in 705 under the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldhelm">Aldhelm</a>. Monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmesbury-abbey">Malmesbury</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glastonbury-abbey">Glastonbury</a> became centres of learning, and Boniface, a West Saxon monk probably born in Devon, worked to convert and organise the church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the eighth century. Trade flowed through the emporium of Hamwic, on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>, which flourished in the eighth century and dealt with the Frankish world. Alfred’s burhs, several planned with regular streets, later became market towns of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, among them Winchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallingford">Wallingford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wareham">Wareham</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> pennies minted in Wessex circulated widely.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun.jpg" alt="Battle of Ellendun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ellandun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Derville Rowlandson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army was raised from the king’s companions and from the free men of the shires, led by ealdormen, and in the early centuries war meant raids and battles against British kingdoms and English rivals. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caedwalla">Cædwalla</a> (r. 685–688) the West Saxons conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a> and Sussex and briefly held Kent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine</a> and his successors pushed into the British kingdom of Dumnonia, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> in the late seventh and eighth centuries. In 825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beornwulf-of-mercia">Beornwulf of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ellendun">Battle of Ellendun</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swindon">Swindon</a>, a victory that brought Kent, Surrey, Sussex and Essex under West Saxon rule, and in 838 he defeated an alliance of Cornish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hingston-down">Hingston Down</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> that landed in 865 conquered Northumbria, East Anglia and much of Mercia, and attacked Wessex in 871, the ‘year of battles’, which included the West Saxon victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ashdown">Ashdown</a>. In January 878 a surprise Danish attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chippenham">Chippenham</a> drove Alfred into the marshes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athelney">Athelney</a>, but in May he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edington">Battle of Edington</a>. Alfred then reorganised the defence of the kingdom: a network of about thirty fortified burhs, listed in the document known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghal-hidage">Burghal Hidage</a>, was garrisoned by men supplied from the surrounding land, the army was divided so that half served while half stayed at home, and new ships were built.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle.jpg" alt="Anglo-Saxon Chronicle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peterborough.Chronicle.firstpage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photographed by Geogre, original author, monastic scribes in Peterborough, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wessex was ruled by kings who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdic">Cerdic</a>, though for much of the seventh century power seems to have been shared among several sub-kings, and succession often passed between distant branches of the royal kin. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine</a> (r. 688–726) issued a law code, probably around 694, the earliest surviving from Wessex, dealing with church dues, theft, wergilds and the duties of farmers, and Alfred later attached it to his own code. By the eighth century the kingdom was divided into shires named after their central places or peoples, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, each under an ealdorman, with royal estates collecting food renders, and the king consulted his councillors, the witan, on law and policy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht</a> (r. 802–839) and his descendants consolidated the kingdom and placed eastern provinces such as Kent under members of the royal family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> (r. 871–899) strengthened royal government: his law book drew on the codes of Ine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>, his burhs created a lasting framework of defence and administration, and he presented himself as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, all the English not under Danish rule. His household served in rotating shifts, as his biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a> described, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a></em> was compiled in his reign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pastoral-care"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pastoral-care.jpg" alt="Pastoral Care" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pastoral-care">Pastoral Care</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01_01B_504_4TroyesMS504PastoralCare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gregory the Great, unknown writer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wessex was the only English kingdom to survive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> conquests of the ninth century, and it became the core of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its royal house ruled England for most of the following century and a half, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> remained a principal royal centre and treasury into the twelfth century. Alfred’s programme of learning had lasting effects on English culture. Lamenting that few priests south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> could understand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, he arranged for books ‘most necessary for all men to know’ to be put into English, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoral-care">Pastoral Care</a></em>, with a preface in his own name; English versions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boethius">Boethius</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-consolation-of-philosophy">Consolation of Philosophy</a></em>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a>’s history and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people">Ecclesiastical History</a></em> were also made in this period. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a></em>, begun in his reign, was continued in several monasteries into the twelfth century and is a principal source for early English history. The West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> dialect became the standard written form of late Old English, in which much Old English literature survives, and Alfred’s burhs set the pattern of many English towns. Earlier, Boniface had helped to organise the church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldhelm">Aldhelm of Malmesbury</a> was among the first English scholars to write extensively in Latin. Alfred remains the only English king to be called ‘the Great’.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians.jpg" alt="Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians">Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Oswald%27s_Priory,_Gloucester_-_geograph.org.uk_-_443171.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philip Halling, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Wessex did not fall; it expanded until it became something larger. After 886, when Alfred occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and entrusted it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians">Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians</a>, ‘all the English people who were not under subjection to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>’ submitted to him, and charters began to call him king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a>, a realm joining Wessex and English Mercia. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> (r. 899–924), working with his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians</a>, built more burhs and conquered the Danish territories south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>: by 918 the armies of East Anglia and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> had submitted, and after Æthelflæd’s death that year Edward took direct control of Mercia. Edward’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>, accepted first in Mercia and then in Wessex, took York from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> in 927, and on 12 July of that year the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, Strathclyde and Bamburgh, and probably Welsh kings, submitted to him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamont-bridge">Eamont</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a>. From then on Æthelstan ruled as king of the English, and Wessex became a region of the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Its name survived in an earldom: in the eleventh century the earls of Wessex, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godwin-earl-of-wessex">Godwin</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold</a>, were among the most powerful men in England, and Harold became king in 1066.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Wessex was the kingdom of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> that alone among the English kingdoms survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> conquests; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> it built burhs, laws and a revival of learning in English, and his son and grandson expanded it into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a> as its royal centre.</em></p>

